WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.030
- A nuclear reactor run
by undergraduate students

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:07.210
at a liberal arts college
sounds like an interesting idea,

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.010
but it's real and it's been here

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.880
at Reed College in Portland,
Oregon for 50 years.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.270
And, yeah, we're gonna put a camera in it.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:15.103
Go for it.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.320
- The Reed Research
Reactor is a TRIGA reactor,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.330
it's designed to be operated by students

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.430
and designed to be used
as a training reactor.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.050
We got it because we had a
radiochemist on Reed's faculty.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.540
He thought that it'd be really important

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.530
for his students to have access

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:35.460
to this cutting edge technology
that was nuclear reactors.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.160
We have a tank that's 25-feet deep.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.900
It's full of around 25
thousand gallons of water.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.500
We think we have enough uranium

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.060
to continue operating at our
current rate for 100 years

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.370
without having to change it out.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.950
We operate at 250 kilowatts,

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.690
which is actually a pretty low power.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.690
While it does produce
power when it operates,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.650
there's no way of harnessing
that to make electricity.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.450
The main purpose of our
reactor is for science.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.680
- The kind of research we do

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.330
is called Neutron Activation Analysis.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:05.320
The logic behind it is
if you don't know exactly

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.500
what something is made out of,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.410
you can make it radioactive and then find

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.720
exactly what it's made out of.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.910
Then, work backwards and figure
out what it was originally.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.820
Then, let it sit in a room until it decays

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.920
and then you have your
original thing back.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.720
You can put 80 things in there.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.040
You drop it into a tube
and then you turn it,

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.120
and it turns and it gets

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.480
the same amount of neutron exposure.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.540
Then, we get it out with a fishing rod.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.080
Then afterwards, you
have to be very careful

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.580
with it because it's contaminated.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.740
The water at the bottom of the reactor

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.060
becomes radioactive, it has N16 in it.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.940
Nitrogen 16 because N16 has
a half-life of seven seconds.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.440
When the water is cycling through,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.100
it gets diffused through a nozzle

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.260
which causes the new water
to swirl around like this,

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.840
which makes the delivery time from

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.360
the water at the bottom of the reactor

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.140
to the water at
the top of the reactor

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.660
just a few seconds longer
than it needs to be.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.100
So, water at the top isn't radioactive.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.780
That's one of the coolest
parts of our water system.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.980
- The radiation levels
that the reactor produces

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.460
is actually very low, but the water
provides sufficient shielding.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.600
So, it's safe for us to be in here

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:08.800
while the reactor's operating.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.200
It's a very small reactor.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.480
It doesn't get hot enough to melt down.
It doesn't explode.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.780
The way we shut down our
reactor in an emergency

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.200
is with something called a SCRAM.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.900
We have a special button, it's
labelled the SCRAM button.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.520
When you press it, it cuts
power to those electromagnets

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.480
and it automatically
shuts the reactor down.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.900
If someone drops their
phone or some other object

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.180
into the pool, normally what we do is

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.080
we get a bucket on some
string and we try to scrape

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.980
the object into the bucket
with a very long stick.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.240
Or, we wrap duct tape around heavy weights

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.760
and we lower the weight
into the pool on a string

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.060
and try to pick it up that way.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.540
We have a pretty good success rate.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.300
- Can we turn the lights off
so we can see the blue glow?

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:49.860
Wow, that looks awesome.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.400
The blue glow is Cherenkov radiation,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.800
which is a bit like the visual
equivalent of a sonic boom,

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.280
but there are other people

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.680
much better qualified to
explain that than I am.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.040
Research reactors aren't very common,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.400
but there are more than you might think.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.220
There are a couple of dozen
in the United States alone

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.880
at universities and research institutes
and a few companies.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.000
This one is basically a student society,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.600
just a student society with
a lot of regulation and oversight.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.580
- Before you're allowed
to operate the reactor,

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.060
you have to get a licence from
the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.860
Our students train for an academic year
before they sit down for that exam.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.480
You have to do things
such as memorise diagrams

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.840
for our cooling system or
our ventilation system.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.120
You need to be able to
explain how the physics work,

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.940
how to protect yourself from radiation

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.200
and how to actually use the
controls of the reactor.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.080
We licence 15 students
to operate every year.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.060
Currently, we have 30 to 40
students on staff.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.900
- You don't get academic
credit for working the reactor.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.340
I think part of that
is because we get paid

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.080
to work here because this is a job.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.060
This is the reason why I came to Reed.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.340
I just thought it was really cool

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.940
that this was a facility
that existed at a school

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.960
and I was like, "I'm gonna do that."

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.500
When I first started
the training programme,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.960
I had pull aside the training supervisors

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.860
and say, "You have to
explain the atom to me

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.000
"from the beginning because
I don't know anything."

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.040
I worked really hard and I
just really wanted to know it.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.120
Now I know it and I still
think it's really cool

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.100
and I get to do cool
things like operate this.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.600
- Thank you so much to everyone
at the Reed Research Reactor

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.420
and, in particular, Val and Carrie

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:19.500
who are putting my camera into it.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.720
Pull down the description for more links

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.720
about Reed College and about the reactor.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.560
I'm looking at a nuclear reactor.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.180
Oh, wow.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.180
I've never actually
seen Cherenkov radiation

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.380
in person before, it's amazing.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.120
in person before, it's amazing.

